Date: Tue, 16 Feb 93 13:10:45 EST
From: Andrew S Hall <ashall@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
Subject: Slave Schools

Not like I am defending our nations gulags (I mean schools), but
does anyone really expect schools to not be mini indoctrination
centers for whatever ideologies their masters have?

I loathed public school, but I would not call it monolithic.
I had teachers who were very diverse. One was a Kennedy liberal freak
(I loved to attack him from Maoist point of view which really
bothered him), one was an ex-Trappist monk who was absolutely
brilliant, and another was a Goldwater conservative. These
examples also leave out the entirely irrelevant beliefs of
a variety of teachers who taught things like chemistry where personal
beliefs never came up.

I will agree that the administration and the concept was entirely
statist and idiotic. I think that all administrators are naturally
conservative in the real sense of the word. Still, as a high school
brat, I would have been against them even if they had been complete
libertarians. At that age, dissent is what is important. I would
be most annoyed if my children were to mindlessly follow what was
given to them by any school or authority figure.

Libertarian or extropian schools would be no better. You would have
90% of the students swallowing the doctrine and 10% who would become
diehard socialists out of spite. Of that 10% most would remain stuck
in that dissenting rut and only a few would emerge with the skills
to *honestly* defend whatever beliefs that they had arrived at.
I don't think you can teach questioning of doctrine. That is
something that has to be arrived at by family, enviroment and other
small scale interactions.

Andrew Hall

BTW- This is *not* a defense of public schools, just a recognition of
youthful human nature. The major problem is that government schools
really suck (despite some really good teachers) at teaching what they
are supposed to. Screw doctrine. I would be happy with *any* school
system that could actually impart knowledge to the students. In
America, private schools are probably the only way. In other countries
that don't have our cultural diversity, the state schools do a really
good job.

