Date: Tue, 16 Feb 93 13:16:57 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Slave Schools

Andrew Hall writes:
.....
>are supposed to. Screw doctrine. I would be happy with *any* school
>system that could actually impart knowledge to the students. In
>America, private schools are probably the only way. In other countries
>that don't have our cultural diversity, the state schools do a really
>good job.

I really have to disagree with this and, if we were following the rules
proposed by Morrow and Sandfort, would ask you to provide some evidence for
this claim (that state schools in other countries do "a really good job.")

Superficially, as measured in terms of standardized test scores, the
schools in several European and Asian nations seem to produce
better-educated graduates, at least from the high school level
("gymnasium"). An ex-girlfriend went to a gymnasium in Denmark and was much
better educated in languages and humanities than her U.S. counterparts (she
spent one year as an exchange student).

But there are several important caveats (aren't there always?):

1. Standardized tests are not everything. For example, they don't measure
creativity very well. Japanese students are strongly indoctrinated in their
state schools to never question authority, to rarely ask questions, and to
basically avoid thinking about things outside the scope of their lessons
(which are often nothing more than cram sessions for their big exam to get
into the right universities).

2. Americans lag the rest of the First World (Europe, Japan) based on test
scores at the elementary and secondary level. By the end of college,
Americans have generally caught up with their counterparts in Europe and
Japan, for various reasons (mentioned below). By the end of grad school,
American-educated students are way ahead (by many measures, and reflected
in the influx of foreign students to American grad programs).

The reasons for this "catching up" are manifold:

* college students are selected for certain skills and desires, not seen in
mandatory high school education (and recall that in other First World
countries, less academically skilled students are routed early on into
vocational training, which tends to skew many of the reports about how
well-educated they are compared to American high school students).

* creativity--the ability to synthesize new ideas from learned
material--becomes more important as educational level rises (at least in
the sciences!). Students who have been brainwashed into believing that "the
nail that stands up gets pounded down," a favorite saying in Japanese
schools, are rarely prepared to make original contributions.

(This is the point on which I disagree most strongly with Andrew Hall: any
attempt to make our primary and seconday schools more Japanese- or
European-like, by emphasing rote learning over creativity and
inquisitiveness, is a move in the _wrong_ direction. If I had any children,
I would certainly want them to grow up loving learning for its own sake,
rather than knowing the formula for the quadratic equation (which I never
once used in my years at Intel or since!). Perhaps this is not the point
Andrew was making, though, so let me not dump on him further! In any case,
the issue of _free choice_ in schooling, as with the relatively free market
in colleges and universities, is the important point.)

By the way, the Japanese have recently realized that their vaunted
educational system has left them relatively unprepared to face the creative
challenges of the future. Look at the number of Japanese Nobel Prize
winners--the few there are, such as Esaki, Yukawa, etc., were mostly
educated in the U.S. Even their touted dominance in the chip business is
seen to be less spectacular than once believed, as Intel has taken the lead
as the world's largest chip company. The Japanese narrowly focussed on
semiconductor DRAMs and missed out on microprocessors, workstations,
personal computers, programmable logic, and lots of other areas.
Ironically, they are now under seige from the South Koreans in memory
chips!

* competition amongst colleges and universities is of course a big factor
(one I won't go into here with you Extropians).

* in Japan, once a student gets into a university, he can slack off. (The
situation is somewhat the same in Germany, I am told) Even at prestigious
Tokyo University, college life is  mostly a big party (several reports on
this have been aired on television and in the press). University research
is of poor quality compared to U.S. university research (a dominant factor
in the grad school situation, of course), and this affects the
undergraduates, one can assume. (Note that most important Japanese research
is done at corporate labs. Next time you get panicky about the reported
dominance of Japanese research labs lie Fujitsu, NEC, and Hitachi, remind
yourself that Japan has essentially no equivalents of Stanford, M.I.T.,
Caltech, Berkeley, and on and on.)

So, the idea that the state schools of Japan and Europe are somehow better
than the state schools of America is mostly refuted. This is not to defend
America's state monopoly (effectively) on schooling. Choice would of course
be more desirable.

-Tim




