Date: Tue, 16 Feb 93 12:36:26 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: DONORS: Anonymity Breaches and Legal Obligations

Tom Morrow's comments about his donor experiences raise some interesting
issues: 

1. Is a "genetic vote" important? (Interesting that Tom also raised the
issue last fall about the importance of one vote in general elections. Much
the same arguments apply to parenthood, except with a vengeance, as genetic
traits are even less correlated to specific contributors---tendency to the
mean and all that.)

2. More importantly, how long will it be before these sperm banks are hit
with lawsuits, despite the contracts presumably signed by all participants?


"All" is misleading, it may be argued, because the offspring of such a
contract did not consent. This has been used in recent years to force
fathers to pay for child support regardless of any agreements made or
contracts signed. Courts have argued that the "welfare of the child" takes
precedence over any contracts signed, and have thus forced fathers to pay
even when it was shown that the mothers lied about birth control measures,
or their infertility, or such.

I'm not a lawyer, but I can see sperm donors being declared "biological
fathers" and hence responsible for partial child support, based as usual in
this country on the "ability to pay." I expect to see some controversial
court cases in the next decade, especially as some clever lawyers decide
this is a new frontier, an untapped market.

Tom mentions the "helpful assistants" (one is forced to wonder just how
"helpful" they were--never mind) and how they steered a couple toward his
donation. This seems to me to be a gross violation of what I presumed would
be a better randomization procedure (partial randomization, with selection
for things like ethnic type, hair color, etc.). If the helpful assistants
know the parentage relationship, they can presumably be ordered to reveal
this to the mother, to the father-seeking child (in 15 or 20 years), or to
the court.

Seems like a big risk for a minuscule gain.

-Tim


