Subject: DONORS: Anonymity Breaches and Legal Obligations

Tim May posts:

>1. Is a "genetic vote" important?

While Tim correctly points out a facial similarity between voting and
donating, there are significant differences between the two activities.  In
the first place, voting is a win/lose proposition; either your candidate
prevails or your vote is for naught.  Reproducing, on the other hand, can
succeed in improving the gene pool to varying degrees.  The similarities
between voting and donating are still weaker once you stop justifying the
latter as merely a means of improving the gene pool.  There are immediate
benefits to donating sperm, both financial (the pay) and psychological
(knowing that you have helped a childless couple and fathered children). 
While one might counter that voting also provides the immediate benefit of
a warm, fuzzy feeling, this feeling is arguably based on a false assessment
on the effect of one's vote. So long as you aim at nothing more modest than
having a few offspring, donating presents no such false assessment problem.

>2. More importantly, how long will it be before these sperm banks are hit
>with lawsuits, despite the contracts presumably signed by all participants?

Almost all activity exposes one to legal risks.  But in California, at
least, the legal risks of donating appear quite small.  This is actually an
area that has seen some litigation, and the law in CA is well established: 
donors are not parents in the eyes of the law; donees are.  In addition to
my own research on this topic, I had occasion, in the course of my career
as a donor, to confirm this interpretation of CA law with one of the
state's leading attorneys in reproduction law.

This is not to say that a donor or bank might not be held liable on other
grounds.  I imagine there would be hell to pay, for example, if one or both
parties intentionally or negligently misprepresented the goods sold.  But
there is nothing special about this sort of suit; it just goes to show that
the stud business is not above the law.

>I'm not a lawyer, but I can see sperm donors being declared "biological
>fathers" and hence responsible for partial child support, based as usual in
>this country on the "ability to pay." I expect to see some controversial
>court cases in the next decade, especially as some clever lawyers decide
>this is a new frontier, an untapped market.

Again, this matter is well settled in CA.  But Tim is right to warn that
the law could change, and nobody should become a donor without being aware
of such risks--especially in states other than CA.

>Tom mentions the "helpful assistants" (one is forced to wonder just how
>"helpful" they were--never mind) and how they steered a couple toward his
>donation. This seems to me to be a gross violation of what I presumed would
>be a better randomization procedure (partial randomization, with selection
>for things like ethnic type, hair color, etc.). If the helpful assistants
>know the parentage relationship, they can presumably be ordered to reveal
>this to the mother, to the father-seeking child (in 15 or 20 years), or to
>the court.

I don't quite understand the claim here.  The assistants were offering the
would-be parents help in finding a donor who would most match the father. 
Nobody wants more randomization than necessary.  Photos--and even
names--would be provided to parents were there not privacy concerns.  The
assistants found a nice way to protect my privacy while helping the parents
around the blindness problem.  No one would have to depose the assistants
about the parentage relationship, as the bank keeps careful records of such
