and goes on to mention speakers and meetings dealing with the same
topic.  While I am all for cutting off Big Brother's allowance, this
sort of dodge is not, IMHO, the way to go about it.  Harry Browne
gives his reasons for avoiding this sort of movement in "How I Found
Freedom..."; I can back his ideas up with a very relevant concrete
example:
 
    One of the organizations promoting this idea of using the 14th
admendment as a means to avoiding taxes was Associate De Libertas
(sp?), ADL for short.  They published books, gave seminars, advised
people on how to deal with the IRS, and generally were quite
successful, eventually opening five or so offices across the US.
Tom (a friend of a friend who had been through the IRS' wringer)
became a true believer, and eventually a manager for ADL.  He often
told us how the IRS weren't at all opposed to ADL's aims, and were
pleased to have some challenges to their legal status brought forth,
etc.  etc.
 
     I got an even better chance to see what ADL was all about in
November during COMDEX, when I ended up staying in ADL's Las Vegas
HQ (a large two story house) at Tom's invitation.  One day late
in the week, I returned to the "office" after spending the day at
COMDEX.  To make a long story short, it is extremely disconcerting to
to find one's residence, even one's temporary residence, surrounded
by men in blue windbreakers marked "FEDERAL AGENT".
 
     Their search warrant allowed them to seize any and all records,
books, computers, and related equipment on the premises, which they
did...  unfortunately, my laptop happened to be in the house, and it
was swept away along with everything else.  Among the "everything
else" was a complete list of all ADL members all over the United
States, who will no doubt be first in line for audits and/or charges
for failing to file this April.
 
     As we found out later, the Las Vegas raid was only part of the
story - the IRS had executed simultaneous raids in at least five
locations in (I *think*) in five different states.  This was an
extremely well-planned event, with significant expenditure of time
and manpower - the feds apparently regarded ADL as a serious threat.
 
     My own personal experiences in the midst of all this are also
pretty disturbing...  it's one thing to read articles in REASON about
people being victimized by confiscation, and quite another to
actually be on the hot seat.  My laptop computer was among the items
seized in the IRS raid in Vegas - after all, it was computer
equipment in the house, and their search warrant allowed them to grab
it.  However, as I explained to far too many people who didn't give a
damn, I'm not a US citizen or resident, I'm not and never was a
member of ADL (US constitutional loopholes aren't too useful up here
in Canada), and my laptop computer had no information on it
relating to ADL in any way, which the Feds themselves verified after
dragging the computer off to Utah...  never mind the fact that I had
*done* *nothing* *wrong* except possibly stay in the same house as
known tax dodgers.
 
     After the raid, I tried every trick I could think of to get the
laptop back...  I browbeat a US Attorney in his own office, I called
the FBI, I threatened everyone in sight with the Canadian embassy,
and finally ended up arguing with two IRS agents in their Vegas HQ
(possibly one of the most futile things I've ever done in my life).
Understand, I'm a big, imposing kinda guy, with enough acting
experience to project very obvious body language that says, "Me alpha
male here; me going to bust heads if little men do not cooperate".  I
tried reasoning, I tried threatening, I tried yelling...  these guys
didn't blink, didn't care, and basically informed me that nobody
could do anything to them; if I didn't like it, tough...  If you want
to see human evil close up, visit your local revenoo-ers...
 
     As you may have guessed, I still don't have my laptop back,
three months after the fact.  I suppose I could go to court, but the
legal fees and travel expenses to do so would greatly exceed the
value of the computer.  I may still get it back, after some extensive
cajoling of the jerk-in-charge, but I'm not getting my hopes up.
 
     The morals of the story are, I suppose:
 
1) Joining a taxfighter group or even being on their mailing list
makes you a fixed target for the IRS.
 
2) IRS raids are not just media hype; they really happen.
 
3) Fighting for what's right can cost big bucks, particularly if the
gov manages to wrong you first and get ahold of some of your
property.
 
4) The IRS, as you may have suspected, isn't really too concerned
with any interpretation of the Constitution other than what is
"commonly accepted" i.e. says that they own your butt.
 
5) Right or wrong, doing something that's going to turn Big Brother's
bleary, bloodshot eye your way is a losing game (I'm tempted to say
zero-sum game :-).  BB is a big ugly hulk, but boy, can he be a
pain in the ass when you've got his attention.
 
     For now...  if anyone's got even crude nano-disassemblers
working, please give me a shout; I have a great application for 'em.
 
