> > little hope of decoding that area any time soon (and I mean several
> > decades at the very least, IMHO). Don't hold your breath for uploading.
> 
> Beep beep beep!  You don't need to speak French to typeset a book in French.
>  What we don't get for a long time is direct i/o with the brain (or its
> uploaded/simulated version) without going through the senses.

But the understanding is necessary before you can think about simplifying the 
process. If you have no understanding of what that brain information represents,
then your hope of extracting the important (algorithmic) features from the
side effects (random firings, failure of certain mechanisms, redundency) is 
very slim. So any hope of uploading 'consciousness' without uploading a 
simulation of the whole brain is slim, and it gives you big problems for fooling
around with the algorithm (say, by changing it to run on a computer without
as many processors as the brain has neurons, and without the same pattern of
connections). Basically understanding of the mechanisms of consciousness is 
essential for any uploading system other than the most brutal brute force
system (make a model of each individual neuron, including all the flaws, and
connect up said neurons with a set of connections the same as that in that 
particular brain). We are a long way of any clear understanding of how the
association cortex, thalamus, limbic system, frontal lobes, and other 
'cognitive' type areas work. 
	I remain an uploading sceptic (at least for any time soon). However
the similar but different scenario of gradually integrating more and more
computer systems within the living brain ( until eventually they are a more
important component) is a bit more feasible.
						Dave Cake
PS The problem with brute force uploading (simulating each individual neuron)
is that we don't have anything like the scanning tech needed to completely 
a neuron without removing it from the living brain. It is much more complicated
than you might think (don't be fooled by overly simplistic neural network
 models).
